+# Packet Plumber on UE — engine-comparison research report
+
+*Job: packet-plumber-ue-bootstrap · 2026-08-20 · Review gate before any
+architecture work. Claims are cited inline; unverifiable statements are
+marked as engineering judgment. Sources at the bottom.*
+
+---
+
+## 1. TL;DR
+
+1. **UE 5.9 is real and AI-first, but not out.** Confirmed in development at
+   Unreal Fest Seoul (2026-08-20): "5.9 will advance AI functionality and
+   mobile optimization even further" (Inven). Release window ~Nov/Dec 2026
+   (briefing; consistent with the ~2-major-per-year cadence). **Scaffold on
+   5.8 now — the official MCP plugin v1 lives there (released 2026-06-17).**
+2. **MCP is production-usable for agent workflows today.** Official Unreal
+   MCP (in-editor, loopback HTTP/SSE) + community UE-MCP (stdio, 783+
+   actions, wraps the official 830-tool registry, guard rails). Wired:
+   **UE-MCP** — verified booting on this machine. UE 6 (late-2027 EA) makes
+   MCP its open-AI integration standard — the choice compounds.
+3. **The determinism spine ports cleanly — and is already proven.** The
+   ODN-9 RNG + FNV-1a-64 + LE serialization + replay-equality port runs
+   green against the Odin reference vectors, engine-free (check count is
+   counted at runtime — the pinned-vector + golden assertions never
+   hardcode a number).
+   UE's gaps (float math, map iteration, engine RNG drift) are avoided by
+   the SAME rules the Odin spine already enforces — port, not adapt.
+4. **Slice-1 recommendation:** prove the comparison axes on the smallest
+   real surface — sim spine + fixed tick + topology with per-hop/ECMP
+   routing + headless automation + ONE MCP-driven screenshot loop + a
+   packaged build size/path readout. ~2-3 story slices; see §6.
+5. **The honest cost:** UE build times (30-60+ min first build) and
+   packaged size (~100MB+ vs raylib MBs) are the Odin build's biggest
+   advantages; agent drivability, tooling maturity, platform reach and the
+   **visual ceiling** are UE's. On the user's stated motivation ("would UE
+   look better?"): Packet Plumber is a 2D top-down puzzle — UE's ceiling
+   (Niagara VFX, MegaLights, Lumen, UMG polish, marketing-grade frames) is
+   far above raylib's flat look, and presentation feeds the GDD's
+   "sell the experience" goal. That is now a first-class axis (§5) with a
+   side-by-side visual probe in slice-2 (same scene, both engines,
+   vision-read + human eyes). The evidence track (this repo) is built to
+   measure both.
+
+---
+
+## 2. UE 5.9 timeline + AI features
+
+**What is confirmed (sourced):**
+
+- **5.8 shipped 2026-06-17** (State of Unreal, Unreal Fest Chicago):
+  MegaLights production-ready, Lumen Lite (Switch 2 + mobile), ~68% fewer
+  shader compilations at runtime, Mesh Terrain; **experimental MCP server
+  support**; the last planned major release of the UE5 line (Digital
+  Trends).
+- **5.9 announced-in-development** at Unreal Fest Seoul 2026-08-20:
+  "advance AI functionality and mobile optimization further" (Inven
+  Global, keynote by Julien Marchand, Senior Director, Framework & Systems
+  Engineering). A live MCP demo drove lighting, materials, Niagara
+  particles, and UMG HUD via natural language + image inputs; outputs were
+  placed as editable meshes/PCG graphs, not images.
+- **UE 6 (after 5.9):** UE5 + UEFN unification; **open AI strategy via MCP
+  (Claude, Codex, Cursor)**, AI agent-driven engine API redesign, USD/glTF
+  portability, Lore (content-addressed VCS, MIT since June 2026), Scene
+  Graph, Verse/STM. **Early Access late 2027, full ~12 months later.**
+- **Epic Developer Assistant** (AI dev assistant, 5.6+ since Sept 2025):
+  the briefing adds it gains project context + screenshots + semantic
+  search in the 5.9 cycle (user-provided detail — not independently
+  verified; consistent with the Seoul keynote's AI-workflow theme).
+
+**Implication for this repo:** 5.8.x is the build engine now; the 5.9
+upgrade is a *planned, low-risk* step (see §7) — and UE 6's MCP-first
+direction de-risks the MCP investment made in 5.8.
+
+---
+
+## 3. MCP landscape — what agents can drive today
+
+### 3.1 Official: Unreal MCP (UE 5.8+, experimental)
+
+In-editor MCP server (plugin id `ModelContextProtocol`, server name
+`unreal-mcp`). Details from Epic's docs (via vrealmatic tracking page):
+
+| Aspect | Status |
+|---|---|
+| Transport | HTTP + SSE, **loopback only** (127.0.0.1:8000 default), **no auth** (rejects non-loopback Origin) |
+| Tools | Toolset Registry: Python/C++ toolsets discovered at startup; **Tool Search mode** (meta-tools `list_toolsets` / `describe_toolset` / `call_tool`) so hundreds of tools don't flood `tools/list` |
+| Shipped toolsets | SceneTools, ActorTools, MaterialInstanceTools, ObjectTools, … — spawn actors, lighting, materials, Slate inspection, **run automation tests**; ~830 tools in the registry per UE-MCP's snapshot |
+| Client config | `ModelContextProtocol.GenerateClientConfig ClaudeCode\|Cursor\|VSCode\|Gemini\|Codex\|All` → writes `.mcp.json` (`"type":"http","url":"http://127.0.0.1:8000/mcp"`) |
+| Start | Editor preference (Auto Start), console `ModelContextProtocol.StartServer`, or CLI `-ModelContextProtocolStartServer` |
+| Extensibility | Author your own toolsets (Python or C++ `ToolsetDefinition`) — studio-specific wrappers; registry is shared with other AI surfaces |
+| Limits | Tools only (no Resources/Prompts yet); serial execution on the game thread; editor-only registry discovery (cooked builds need `AddTool()`); Live Coding won't pick up new UFUNCTIONs |
+
+**What agents can't drive today (official):** no stdio transport, no
+auth (loopback-only discipline), no async/parallel calls, viewport
+screenshots are not a first-class shipped tool (5.9's expanded plugin +
+EDA screenshots are the roadmap), and it needs the editor running.
+
+### 3.2 Community: UE-MCP (db-lyon/ue-mcp, MIT)
+
+TypeScript/Node stdio server → WebSocket JSON-RPC bridge (C++ plugin)
+inside the editor; **filesystem reads (INI, C++ headers, asset listing)
+work with the editor closed**.
+
+| Aspect | Status |
+|---|---|
+| Surface | 24 category tools, **783+ actions**; on 5.8+ wraps Epic's native Toolset Registry in-process (`epic_*` actions, 830 tools) |
+| Editor lifecycle | `editor(start_editor/stop_editor/restart_editor)` (macOS included); `npx ue-mcp init` deploys the bridge plugin + client config; `npx ue-mcp doctor` |
+| Flows | YAML multi-step flows (`ue-mcp.yml`, hot-reloaded), rollback, retries, shell steps |
+| Safety | **write guards, source-control guard, flow rollback, idempotency by convention** |
+| Requirements | UE 5.6+ macOS (5.4-5.8 Windows), Node 18+, C++ toolchain, PythonScriptPlugin |
+| Maturity | Experimental (created 2026-02), fast-moving (271 stars, 1452 commits by the author); MIT |
+
+### 3.3 The comparison + what we wired
+
+| Criterion | Official Unreal MCP | UE-MCP (community) |
+|---|---|---|
+| Agent-friendly transport | HTTP/SSE (needs editor up) | **stdio — works in any agent harness incl. pi today** |
+| Editor closed? | no | **partial — file/asset reads yes** |
+| Depth | core editor ops; growing | 783+ actions + wraps official registry; Blueprints/materials/PCG/GAS/networking/sequencer |
+| Safety rails | none (loopback only) | write guards + SCM guard + rollback |
+| Longevity | Epic-backed; 5.9/6.0 roadmap | MIT community; risk = bus factor (mitigated: it wraps the official registry, so the official path is the fallback) |
+| Runtime cost | free (in engine) | Node server + bridge compile on first launch |
+
+**Decision D3 (wired):** `.mcp.json` at the repo root registers **UE-MCP**
+(stdio). Rationale: (a) it works with pi's MCP adapter *today* — verified
+booting (27 tools + 1615 flow tasks, Epic 5.8 registry snapshot surfaced);
+(b) editor-closed reads let agents explore the project before the editor
+starts; (c) guard rails make unattended agent use safer. The official
+plugin is enabled in-project and one `GenerateClientConfig` away; the
+5.9/6.0 native path makes the official server the long-term default.
+
+**Screenshot loop (the T2 equivalent):** `editor.capture_screenshot`
+(viewport / pie / window targets) → PNG on disk → `bin/vision-read`
+(the repo's vision entry point — backend-configured or the pi `read`
+tool; flash/glm have no native vision) → compare/diff. Recipe in
+`docs/agent-playbook.md`. Engine-gated: recorded once the editor runs here.
+
+---
+
+## 4. Determinism in UE vs the ODN spine
+
+**The port already works.** `tests/build/spine-check` compiles the SAME
+headers the engine module uses (PP_Rng/PP_Hash/PP_Sim — deliberately
+UE-header-free) and asserts the Odin pinned vectors byte-exact. That is
+the cross-language conformance test (ODN-9, arch REVIEW m6) passing before
+the engine is even installed.
+
+| ODN spine requirement | UE reality | Port decision |
+|---|---|---|
+| ODN-9 owned PRNG, pinned vectors | `FRandomStream` (SRand-based, thread-safe, seedable — engine API) but engine-internal, drifts across versions | **Port PP::FRng (PCG32 XSH-RR + splitmix64)** — proven byte-identical; engine RNG never enters sim state paths |
+| ODN-10 integer math | C++ `uint32/64` arithmetic is bit-identical across targets (IEEE float mostly too, but SIMD/vectorized paths can vary; UE's ParallelFor is unordered) | Integer-only state paths; no `ParallelFor`/async in sim; floats stay in the view (same rule as Odin) |
+| ODN-10 array-only iteration | UE `TMap`/`TSet` iteration order is hash-based/unspecified; `TArray` is insertion-ordered | Arrays + insertion order in sim state paths; maps for lookups only |
+| ODN-2 fixed 20 Hz tick | `bUseFixedFrameRate` + `FixedFrameRate` engine settings; explicit tick stepping is fully portable | Both: config pins 20 Hz for PIE; `PP::Step(Tick)` advances sim explicitly — never depends on frame timing |
+| ODN-11 LE binary save + versioned header | UE `FArchive`/`FMemoryWriter` is deterministic on-platform but its layout is engine-versioned | **Explicit field-by-field LE writer** (ported) — keeps the cross-runner golden contract and 5.9-proofs the format |
+| ODN-11 replay equality | Engine Replay System (UDemoNetDriver) targets networked demos — heavier, netcode-coupled | Action-log re-step (ODN-11) — simpler, byte-exact, runs in automation tests |
+| ODN-17 golden images | Viewport rendering is GPU-dependent (not bit-stable across GPUs/drivers) | Harness equivalent = **MCP screenshot + vision-read** (semantic, not pixel-bit) for editor visuals; the SIM stays hash-verified. Pixel-bit goldens on the sim side are never needed — the sim doesn't render. |
+
+**What is genuinely harder in UE (judgment, grounded in the port):**
+(a) build/cook times make the fast loop 10-100x slower than Odin's
+(~1s gates); (b) the editor binary is the test host — headless runs still
+boot the engine (~30-120s); (c) any future Blueprint-adjacent logic must
+be re-verified against the C++ spine. None of these break the spine; they
+change the iteration economics — which is precisely what the comparison
+track measures.
+
+---
+
+## 5. Comparison criteria (the six axes)
+
+Scored for the evidence track. E = engineering judgment, C = cited.
+
+| Axis | Odin v2 (reference) | UE 5.8 (this repo) | Who wins |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| **Agent drivability** | CLI-only (no editor to drive); diffable text assets | MCP (2 servers) drives editor/assets/builds/screenshots; C++-first diffable; EDA on 5.9 | **UE** (C) |
+| **Iteration speed** | ~1s gates; odin build seconds; hot-reload trivial | First build 30-60+ min, incremental 5-15 min; Live Coding (non-API changes); editor boot ~30-120s | **Odin** (E) |
+| **Headless testability** | Harness with bit-exact T1 hashes + T2 sw pixels | Automation Tests + commandlets (`-run=PPProbe`) headless; sim hash-verified; visuals via MCP screenshots + vision-read | **Odin** for bit-exact; **UE adequate** (E) |
+| **Build times** | seconds (incremental) | 30-60+ min first; 5-15 min incremental; measured per gate in `docs/timings.md` | **Odin** (E) |
+| **Binary size** | raylib native, ~1-10 MB, one binary | Packaged ~100-500 MB+ (engine + content), per-platform | **Odin** (E) |
+| **Platform reach** | Desktop (Win/mac/Linux) + DIY web (wasm)/mobile | PC/Mac/Linux/iOS/Android/consoles incl. Switch 2 (Lumen Lite); mobile-first workflows | **UE** (C) |
+| **Visual quality ceiling** (the "would it look better" axis) | raylib flat 2D — functional, minimal; polish = art + code | Niagara VFX, MegaLights, Lumen (even for 2D-adjacent presentation), UMG UI polish, post-FX, marketing-grade frames | **UE** (E — measurable only by the slice-2 visual probe) |
+
+**Verdict shape (hypothesis, to be evidenced by slice-1/2):** UE wins agent
+drivability, platform reach, tooling maturity, and the visual ceiling;
+Odin wins iteration speed, binary size, and headless rigor. For a
+solo-agent-driven indie pipeline the question is which loss is acceptable —
+the evidence track decides.
+
+---
+
+## 6. Recommended slice-1 scope (the comparison build)
+
+Goal: exercise every comparison axis on the smallest real surface. ~2-3
+story slices:
+
+1. **Slice 1 — sim spine in the engine.** PP::Step driven at 20 Hz by a
+   GameMode/Subsystem; topology (nodes/links) + per-hop forwarding + ECMP
+   (pure splitmix64 hash) + bundles (static-sum capacity) — the routing
+   4-rule port; automation tests: routing determinism + replay equality +
+   RNG vectors; PPProbe extended to dump a routing table. *(Proves:
+   headless testability, determinism, C++-first iteration.)*
+2. **Slice 2 — the agent loop + visual probe.** UE-MCP bridge deployed (`npx ue-mcp
+   init`); one MCP-driven editor session recorded: spawn a node actor
+   grid, screenshot, vision-read verification; a `ue-mcp.yml` flow for
+   build+probe. **Visual probe:** the SAME scene (node grid + packets +
+   crisis FX) rendered in both engines — UE via MCP screenshots, Odin via
+   its golden harness — compared by vision-read + the user's eyes. *(Proves:
+   agent drivability, screenshot loop, visual ceiling.)*
+3. **Slice 3 — package + measure.** UAT BuildCookRun for macOS dev +
+   shipping; record binary size, build time, first-launch time; document
+   the mobile/console story. *(Proves: build times, binary size, platform
+   reach — the Odin comparison numbers.)*
+
+Gate: this report's user review. Out of slice-1: era/economy, crisis
+systems, art, audio, UI (the Odin build's later slices port after the
+engine decision).
+
+---
+
+## 7. The 5.8 → 5.9 upgrade path
+
+1. **Now (5.8.x):** project pinned via `EngineAssociation: "5.8"` in
+   `PacketPlumberUE.uproject`; official MCP plugin enabled; all sim code
+   is 5.9-agnostic by construction (pure C++17, no deprecated APIs).
+2. **When 5.9 ships (~Nov/Dec 2026):** install via the launcher →
+   right-click the .uproject → **Switch Unreal Engine version** → 5.9 →
+   rebuild. UE upgrades are minor-version in-place; the standard risk is
+   plugin/API deprecations, not project migration (engineering judgment;
+   Epic's standard upgrade flow, see versluis + UhiyamaLab guides).
+3. **5.9-specific wins to re-probe:** expanded official MCP plugin
+   (screenshots, EDA w/ project context + semantic search per the
+   briefing) — re-run §3's comparison; the official server likely becomes
+   the default wiring.
+4. **Belt:** keep `docs/timings.md` + golden files as the regression
+   evidence across the version switch; the standalone spine-check must
+   stay green before and after (it doesn't depend on the engine at all).
+
+---
+
+## 8. Decisions the user should rule on (this review)
+
+1. **MCP wiring:** accept UE-MCP (community) as the wired server, official
+   enabled-but-dormant? (Recommend: yes.)
+2. **Slice-1 scope:** approve §6's 3-slice comparison scope?
+3. **Engine install timing:** install UE 5.8 on this machine now (manual
+   ~10 min + 40-60 GB download, then the full CI loop + first timings), or
+   defer?
+4. **LFS adoption:** confirm git-lfs for uassets (needs `git lfs install`
+   once on this machine).
